1017 #edb/ref 17592186265588 13194139533915 true] [17592186265587 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265591 13194139533915 true] [17592186265587 1089 "q-275804" 13194139533915 true] [17592186265587 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533915 true] [17592186265587 1100 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt{3}k,-k),$ where $k$ is a positive real number. Find the polar coordinates of $P$ expressed in terms of $k.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nExpressed in terms of $k,$ the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533915 true] [17592186265587 1113 #uuid "8110ff45-2d78-5e50-8037-bbc031f60791" 13194139533915 true] [17592186265587 1145 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3}k)^{2}+(-k)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{k}^{2}+{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-k}{\\sqrt{3}k})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{\\pi }{6}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point lies in the fourth quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2k,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$" 13194139533915 true] [17592186265588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265589 13194139533915 true] [17592186265588 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265590 13194139533915 true] [17592186265588 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533915 true] [17592186265588 1041 "field-1" 13194139533915 true] [17592186265588 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265589 13194139533915 true] [17592186265588 1110 #uuid "7aa62196-bd64-54d0-acd7-3633862596df" 13194139533915 true] [17592186265589 10